    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a bit whimsical for a formal lecture, but thanks for the basic info. Could you get more specific about the enzymatic process? I need to explain the role of alliinase and how the cell structure breakdown facilitates the release of the sulfoxides. I'm looking for the actual biochemical pathway.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! When you cut an onion, you're essentially breaking the cell walls, which releases enzymes and other compounds. The key enzyme involved in this process is alliinase, also known as LAE. Alliinase is a cysteine protease that catalyzes the conversion of the amino acid S-allyl-L-cysteine sulfoxide (SACSO) into sulfenic acid (SOH), which is highly unstable and immediately rearranges into the more stable sulfoxides, including syn-propanethial-S-oxide.\n\nHere's a more detailed look at the biochemical pathway:\n\n1. Onion cells contain S-allyl-L-cysteine sulfoxide (SACSO), which is a non-volatile compound.\n2. When the onion is cut, the cell walls are disrupted, and the enzyme alliinase is released into the surrounding tissue.\n3. Alliinase catalyzes the conversion of SACSO to sulfenic acid (SOH) and ammonia (NH3).\n4. The sulfenic acid quickly rearranges into sulfinic acid (SO2H) and then into the more stable sulfoxide (SO2).\n5. The sulfoxide formed, syn-propanethial",
      "source": "target",
